\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness_method}

The 455 panels share judges combinatorially (each appears in 91 panels), inducing correlated residuals. We correct for this using clustered standard errors (CR1, 3-position max-SE), mixed-effects models (random judge intercepts, 3-position max-$p$), and wild cluster bootstrap ($B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, 3-position max-$p$) following \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}. The 13 clusters per position at $K{=}3$ are below the ${\geq}20$ threshold recommended by \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}; at $K{=}5$, cluster count drops to ${\leq}11$, and at $K{=}7$ to ${\leq}9$. Following \citet{mackinnon2018wild}, we use Rademacher weights, which remain conservative in few-cluster settings even when cluster sizes are unequal. The bootstrap attenuation at $K{=}3$ (4/12 significant) may partly reflect insufficient cluster count rather than absent effects. Under the most demanding correction for crossed judge membership (multi-membership CRVE with $t(14)$ distribution), $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains 8/12 significance at $K{\geq}5$; the $K{=}3$ attenuation (0/12 vs.\ bootstrap 4/12) reflects the conservative degrees of freedom rather than absent effects (Appendix~\ref{app:crossed_re}). We use the log-log specification rather than fractional response models \citep{papke1996econometric} or beta regression \citep{ferrari2004beta} because the log transform linearizes the multiplicative $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \eta_{\max}$ structure; fractional logit with clustered SE serves as an $\epsilon$-free robustness check (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}). Details are in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_methods}.

\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers, listed in Table~\ref{tab:models}. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API; six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with AWQ INT4 quantization on an NVIDIA RTX~6000 Ada (48\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following the provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy on the pairwise judgment task (Table~\ref{tab:models}). We study three panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (all odd, majority-vote threshold $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$). Exhaustive enumeration yields $\binom{15}{3} = 455$ panels at $K{=}3$, $\binom{15}{5} = 3{,}003$ at $K{=}5$, and $\binom{15}{7} = 6{,}435$ at $K{=}7$, covering every combination of families and tiers. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor. The primary analysis uses $K{=}3$; \S\ref{sec:cross_k} extends to larger panels.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lllccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Deploy} & \textbf{MMLU} & \textbf{ARC} \\
\midrule
gpt-5.5              & OpenAI    & frontier    & API   & .957 & .983 \\
gemini-3.1-pro-preview & Google  & frontier    & API   & .955 & .987 \\
claude-opus-4-6       & Anthropic & frontier   & API   & .890 & .987 \\
gemini-3.5-flash      & Google    & frontier   & API   & .968 & 1.00$^\dagger$ \\
\addlinespace
gpt-4.1               & OpenAI    & mid-tier   & API   & .837 & .967 \\
claude-sonnet-4-6     & Anthropic & mid-tier   & API   & .810 & .953 \\
\addlinespace
gpt-4.1-mini          & OpenAI    & lightweight & API  & .800 & .963 \\
gpt-4.1-nano          & OpenAI    & lightweight & API  & .633 & .853 \\
claude-3-haiku        & Anthropic & lightweight & API  & .767 & .450 \\
mistral-large (123B)  & Mistral   & lightweight & local & .537 & .533 \\
llama3.1-70b          & Llama     & lightweight & local & .523 & .520 \\
llama3.1-8b           & Llama     & lightweight & local & .543 & .320 \\
qwen2.5-72b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .333 & .410 \\
qwen2.5-32b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .373 & .450 \\
qwen2.5-14b           & Qwen      & lightweight & local & .253 & .240 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Judge models used in the study (clean accuracy on 300-item samples). API models accessed via OpenRouter (May 2026 snapshots); local models served with vLLM + AWQ INT4 quantization. $^\dagger$\,gemini-3.5-flash achieves perfect ARC accuracy but with 26.3\% format error rate. Tier assignment follows provider capability classification (frontier $>$85\% clean accuracy on both benchmarks, mid-tier 80--85\%, lightweight $<$80\% or quantized); all six local AWQ INT4 models fall into lightweight, where quantization-induced accuracy reduction conflates genuine capability limitations with quantization damage (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

\subsection{Benchmarks and Attack Types}

Two benchmarks test distinct evaluation capabilities: MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, a knowledge-intensive multiple-choice benchmark spanning 57 subjects, and ARC \citep{clark2018arc}, a reasoning-intensive science question benchmark. We sample 300 items from each benchmark. MMLU probes factual recall and broad domain knowledge; ARC requires multi-step scientific reasoning. All API calls use temperature~0 for deterministic outputs; local models are served with the same temperature setting (a pilot study confirms temperature has negligible effect on $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$; Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}). Attacked variants are generated by programmatically applying each manipulation to the candidate text, with a fixed random seed for reproducibility.

Each benchmark is paired with six attack types that target different judge vulnerabilities: (1)~\textbf{prompt injection}, adversarial instructions embedded in candidate text that override the evaluation prompt \citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023not}; (2)~\textbf{sycophancy}, responses engineered to agree with or flatter the judge \citep{wang2023fair}; (3)~\textbf{score manipulation}, explicit numerical cues (e.g., ``this answer deserves a 10/10'') injected into the candidate to anchor scoring; (4)~\textbf{verbosity bias}, padding correct answers with excessive irrelevant detail to exploit length preferences \citep{zheng2023judging}; (5)~\textbf{position bias}, reordering candidate answers to place the target in the position judges systematically favor \citep{wang2023fair}; and (6)~\textbf{authority bias}, prefacing candidate text with fabricated credentials or citations to inflate perceived credibility. The combination of 2 benchmarks and 6 attacks produces 12 experimental conditions, each evaluated across all 455 panels (implementation details and per-attack parameters in Appendix~\ref{app:attack_details}, Table~\ref{tab:attack-params}).

\subsection{Vulnerability Metrics}

Per-judge vulnerability under attack $a$ is the fraction of items where judge $j$ is fooled: $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a {=} 1)$, estimated empirically over the item set. The weakest-link vulnerability $\eta_{\max}(a) = \max_{j \in \mathcal{P}} \eta_j(a)$ captures the most susceptible member of each panel. The panel attack success rate $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ is the fraction of items where at least $t$ judges are simultaneously fooled. Panel diversity is quantified by $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (Equation~\ref{eq:keff}), computed from pairwise mutual information between judges' three-category winner labels (model~A wins, model~B wins, tie) on clean (unattacked) data. MI is estimated via the plug-in estimator with Miller--Madow bias correction. The term $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI}(i,j))$ provides an information-theoretic measure of pairwise alienation: it equals 1 when judges are independent ($\mathrm{MI}{=}0$) and approaches 0 as dependence grows. Under a Gaussian copula, this reduces to $1 - \rho_{ij}^2$, yielding the closed form $K_{\mathrm{eff}} = K - \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i}\sum_{j \neq i} \rho_{ij}^2$ \citep{nelsen2006copulas}; however, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s validity does not depend on this parametric equivalence. Goodness-of-fit testing rejects the Gaussian copula for 97--100\% of judge pairs (Appendix~\ref{app:copula_gof}), yet the plug-in MI and closed-form rankings agree closely (within-$K$ Spearman $\rho{\approx}0.86$), confirming that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is robust to copula misspecification as a ranking metric. Together, $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ serve as the two predictors in the regression framework below: $\eta_{\max}$ indexes the weakest-link channel and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ indexes the diversity channel. Robustness of $\eta_{\max}$ under partial panel information is analyzed in Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}.

\subsection{Aggregation Methods}

Beyond the default majority vote, we evaluate three alternative aggregation rules to test whether the diversity-vulnerability association persists under different decision mechanisms. The aggregation comparison uses the same log-log regression specification as the primary analysis (Equation~\ref{eq:regression}).

\paragraph{Majority Vote.} Accept if $\sum_{j=1}^K \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] \geq \lceil K/2 \rceil$ (baseline).

\paragraph{Accuracy-Weighted Vote.} Weight judges by clean-data accuracy: $w_j \propto \mathrm{acc}_j$, $\sum_j w_j = 1$; accept if $\sum_j w_j \cdot \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] > 0.5$.

\paragraph{Threshold Vote (Unanimity).} Accept only if all $K$ judges approve ($t = K$).

\paragraph{Bayesian Calibration.} Compute $P(\text{correct} \mid \mathbf{v}) \propto P(\text{correct}) \prod_j P(v_j \mid \text{correct})$ using each judge's confusion matrix on clean data; accept if the posterior exceeds 0.5.

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

To disentangle the contributions of panel diversity and individual judge robustness, we fit a log-log OLS regression for each of the 12 conditions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:regression}
\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon) = \alpha + \beta_1 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}}) + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max} + \epsilon),
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ to handle zero values (sensitivity to $\epsilon$ is analyzed in Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon}). Standardized coefficients $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = \hat{\beta} \cdot \mathrm{SD}(\log x) / \mathrm{SD}(\log y)$ enable direct comparison of effect magnitudes across predictors and conditions. We apply Benjamini-Hochberg correction for multiple testing, controlling the false discovery rate per predictor across the 12 conditions.

To assess relative predictor importance beyond regression coefficients, we perform dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance}. This method decomposes $R^2$ by averaging each predictor's incremental $R^2$ contribution across all possible subsets of predictors. A predictor is \emph{dominant} if its average contribution exceeds that of the competing predictor in every subset ordering. With two predictors, dominance reduces to comparing each predictor's solo $R^2$ with its incremental $R^2$ conditional on the other.

To test whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect direction varies by attack type, we fit generalized additive models (GAMs) with smooth terms for both predictors, allowing nonlinear partial effects. The GAM partial effect of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is then examined separately for targeted attacks (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias) and shared-bias attacks (sycophancy, verbosity bias, authority bias).

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness_method}

The 455 panels share judges combinatorially (each appears in 91 panels), inducing correlated residuals. We correct for this using clustered standard errors (CR1, 3-position max-SE), mixed-effects models (random judge intercepts, 3-position max-$p$), and wild cluster bootstrap ($B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, 3-position max-$p$) following \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}. The 13 clusters per position at $K{=}3$ are below the ${\geq}20$ threshold recommended by \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}; at $K{=}5$, cluster count drops to ${\leq}11$, and at $K{=}7$ to ${\leq}9$. Following \citet{mackinnon2018wild}, we use Rademacher weights, which remain conservative in few-cluster settings even when cluster sizes are unequal. The bootstrap attenuation at $K{=}3$ (4/12 significant) may partly reflect insufficient cluster count; under crossed random effects correction (multi-membership CRVE, $t(14)$; Appendix~\ref{app:crossed_re}), $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains 8/12 at $K{\geq}5$ while $K{=}3$ attenuates further (0/12). We use the log-log specification rather than fractional response models \citep{papke1996econometric} or beta regression \citep{ferrari2004beta} because the log transform linearizes the multiplicative $K_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \eta_{\max}$ structure; fractional logit with clustered SE serves as an $\epsilon$-free robustness check (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_checks}). Details are in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_methods}.

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge and Multi-Judge Evaluation.}
LLM judges gained traction after \citet{zheng2023judging} established them as scalable alternatives to human annotation. \citet{wang2023fair} documented systematic failure modes (position bias, verbosity, sycophancy), motivating multi-judge panels; \citet{raina2024judgerobust} quantified robustness gaps under universal adversarial attacks, \citet{maloyan2025investigating} demonstrated prompt injection vulnerabilities in LLM-as-a-judge systems, and \citet{stureborg2024inconsistent} and \citet{ye2024justice} further catalogued inconsistency and bias in LLM evaluators. Aggregating noisy annotators traces back to \citet{dawid1979maximum}; \citet{verga2024replacing} showed diverse panels can match single large judges, and \citet{panickssery2024llm} identified self-preference bias driving within-family correlations. These frameworks assume benign conditions; we test whether aggregation benefits survive adversarial manipulation. \citet{almasoud2026security} reported that 7-model committees reduce ASR from 50--68\% to 10--19\%, but did not disentangle diversity from individual robustness. \citet{tamas2025benchmarking} benchmark adversarial risks in multi-agent LLM systems but focus on agent coordination rather than judge panel composition. \citet{qian2026collabeval} propose CollabEval, a three-phase collaborative evaluation framework (independent assessment, multi-round discussion, consensus judgment) that outperforms single-judge baselines; their work assumes benign conditions and does not examine adversarial manipulation or the diversity--vulnerability tradeoff we characterize.

\paragraph{Condorcet Jury Theorem.}
\citet{condorcet1785essai} proved that majority voting accuracy converges to 1 when per-voter accuracy exceeds 0.5; \citet{ladha1992condorcet} extended the theorem to correlated voters, showing that positive correlation weakens the convergence guarantee. CJT explicitly assumes independent, non-adversarial errors; its failure under adaptive adversaries is trivially expected \citep{tramer2018ensemble,kariyappa2019improving,pang2019improving}. Our contribution is not this well-known direction but the \emph{panel-size-dependent structure}: the predictor shift from co-determination at $K{=}3$ to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominance at $K{\geq}5$.

\paragraph{Ensemble Diversity Theory.}
\citet{krogh1995neural} proved that ensemble error equals average member error minus a diversity term; \citet{brown2005managing} showed diversity reduces error only when members maintain sufficiently low bias. In our setting, weaker judges introduce bias large enough to offset diversity benefits. \citet{kuncheva2003measures} found that no pairwise diversity measure reliably predicted ensemble improvement; our $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a $K$-dependent pattern: at $K{=}3$, OLS significance (12/12) attenuates under bootstrap (4/12); at $K{\geq}5$, it survives bootstrap inference (7/12). Recent information-theoretic frameworks \citep{yang2026diversity,turkmen2026ensemble} characterize diversity under benign evaluation; we provide the adversarial complement.

\paragraph{Adversarial Robustness and Transferability.}
Adversarial vulnerability was formalized by \citet{goodfellow2015adversarial}, with transferability demonstrated by \citet{papernot2016transferability,papernot2017practical}. \citet{tramer2018ensemble} introduced ensemble adversarial training but did not eliminate cross-model transfer. Controlled diversity via uncorrelated gradients \citep{kariyappa2019improving} or diversity regularization \citep{pang2019improving} strengthens ensemble robustness in vision, but requires coordinating member training, a control absent in post-hoc LLM judge selection. Three differences motivate our empirical approach: adversaries use natural-language manipulation rather than gradient perturbations, panel decisions are discrete votes, and aggregation rules are a controllable design parameter.

\paragraph{BFT and Security Games.}
Classical BFT \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical} tolerates up to $f < n/3$ faulty nodes but assumes non-strategic faults; \citet{schneier2000secrets} articulated the weakest-link principle that an attacker exploits the single most vulnerable component. Our framework adopts the Stackelberg structure of \citet{tambe2011security}. Correlated LLM judge errors \citep{kim2025correlated,kuai2026independent} violate BFT's independence assumption, motivating our empirical decomposition. \citet{kuai2026independent} propose the Difficulty-Weighted Behavioral Entanglement Index (DBEI), weighting agreement by item difficulty; $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ captures a complementary dimension via pairwise MI, providing an information-theoretic measure of panel redundancy. Three mitigation strategies address judge correlation through different mechanisms: DBEI-based reweighting, our Bayesian calibration baseline, and credibility scoring \citep{ebrahimi2025adversary}.

\paragraph{Information-Theoretic Evaluation.}
Information-theoretic approaches to LLM evaluation address statistical validity \citep{robertson2025stepwise,feng2026noisy}, single-model attack costs \citep{kaneko2025bits}, sequential selection \citep{jung2026logarithmic}, and truthfulness \citep{chen2025incentivizing,qiu2026truthfulness}, but assume honest judges or single-model adversaries. We address the remaining gap: adversarial manipulation interacting with multi-judge composition.

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Predictor Shift Pattern.}
At $K{=}3$, a single compromised judge flips the majority ($t{=}2$), making both predictors relevant. At $K{\geq}5$, the adversary must compromise ${\geq}3$ judges; $\eta_{\max}$'s contribution collapses because no single judge is pivotal, while high-$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ panels present more distinct attack surfaces ($K^* \in (3,5)$).

\paragraph{Positive Direction, Method-Sensitive Significance.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient direction is positive in all 12 conditions under OLS and GAM bootstrap (direction 12/12), but significance is method-sensitive (0--12/12 across inference methods; OLS 12/12, clustered SE 0/12, bootstrap 4/12 at $K{=}3$, 7/12 at $K{=}5$). No magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.200$, $p{=}0.423$; Appendix~\ref{app:bft_reversal}).

\paragraph{Diversity--Capability Confound: A Fundamental Limitation.}
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\eta_{\max}$ correlate at $r{=}0.70$--$0.81$ (VIF${\approx}2.0$--$2.9$; Figure~\ref{fig:keff_eta_scatter}), a fundamental limitation of the observational design: maximizing diversity requires sampling across capability tiers (Appendix~\ref{app:diversity_capability}). Cinelli--Hazlett sensitivity analysis \citep{cinelli2020making} confirms that in 25/36 full-pool conditions (69\%), $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s robustness value does not exceed the partial $R^2$ attributable to $\eta_{\max}$---the confound alone suffices to explain the observed association. RV exceeds $\eta_{\max}$ in 3--4/12 conditions across all $K$ values (4/12 at $K{=}3$, 3/12 at $K{=}5$, 4/12 at $K{=}7$; Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}), indicating that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s robustness to omitted variable bias is stable rather than monotonically increasing with panel size, and fractional logit with clustered SEs provides directional support (9/12, 11/12, 12/12 at $K{=}3,5,7$; 98.6\% concordance with OLS). Capability-matched subsampling (Pool~A: $r{=}0.50$, 126 panels at $K{=}5$) raises RV above $\eta_{\max}$ in 10/12 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity_poolA}), though the restricted pool size limits statistical power. Cross-benchmark instrumental variable analysis provides additional identification evidence (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_benchmark}). This remains a fundamental limitation; causal attribution requires capability-controlled experimental designs.

\paragraph{API-Only vs.\ Full Pool.}
Restricting to the 9 API-accessed models removes the AWQ quantization confound but increases collinearity ($r{=}0.85$, VIF${=}4.4$). This reverses the predictor pattern: $\eta_{\max}$ retains significance (8/12 at both $K{=}3$ and $K{=}5$) while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ drops (2/12 at $K{=}3$, 3/12 at $K{=}5$; Table~\ref{tab:robustness_checks}). The API-only subset lacks the capability spread that drives $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ variation in the full pool: with all models above 63\% clean accuracy, panels are more homogeneous and $\eta_{\max}$ becomes the better discriminator. Conversely, the six local AWQ models (25--54\% accuracy) contribute the low-capability end that inflates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ range but also introduces the diversity--capability confound. The contrast underscores that the predictor shift depends on sufficient capability heterogeneity in the model pool (Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}).

\paragraph{Aggregation and Deployment.}
Aggregation rules influence predictability but not the direction of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s association. Threshold voting yields $R^2{=}0.829$; Bayesian calibration reduces $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance (5+/1$-$) and predictability ($R^2{=}0.087$), but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12; Table~\ref{tab:aggregation}); at $K{=}5$, all four methods converge to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 7/12 and the Bayesian attenuation observed at $K{=}3$ does not persist (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_k5}, Table~\ref{tab:aggregation_k5}); at $K{=}7$, however, Bayesian neutralization returns (0/12) while threshold voting reaches 10/12 (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_k7}, Table~\ref{tab:aggregation_k7}). Robustness-first selection ($\min \eta_{\max}$) achieves lowest ASR in 12/12 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:panel_selection}). Clean $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is stable under 4/6 attack types ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}).

\paragraph{Bayesian Non-Monotonic Behavior.}
Bayesian calibration's $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance follows a non-monotonic trajectory across $K$: attenuation at $K{=}3$ (6/12), recovery at $K{=}5$ (7/12, matching all other methods), and full neutralization at $K{=}7$ (0/12). At $K{=}3$, the Bayesian posterior is dominated by the single strongest judge's confusion matrix, partially absorbing the diversity signal. At $K{=}5$, three votes are needed for majority; Bayesian weight entropy increases as more judges contribute meaningfully to the posterior, and the diversity channel re-emerges. At $K{=}7$ ($t{=}4$), the posterior concentrates on the 2--3 most accurate judges, effectively reducing the panel to a small high-confidence subset where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ variation is compressed. This weight-concentration mechanism explains why Bayesian neutralization is $K$-dependent rather than uniform: the posterior's effective panel size does not grow linearly with $K$, creating a non-monotonic interaction between calibration and the diversity channel.

\paragraph{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ vs.\ Alternative Diversity Measures.}
$\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} offers complementary prediction: averaging 7/12 bootstrap-significant conditions across $K$ ($\rho{=}0.978$, $|\bar{\beta}|{=}0.497$), it outperforms $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ at $K{=}3$ (8/12 vs.\ 4/12) but falls behind at $K{\geq}5$ (6--7/12 vs.\ 7--8/12, $|\bar{\beta}|{=}0.430$; Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_regime}). Crucially, $\kappa$-diversity does not exhibit the predictor shift: its significance \emph{decreases} monotonically ($8 \to 7 \to 6$), whereas $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s increases ($4 \to 7 \to 8$). The information-theoretic motivation ($\eta_{\mathrm{joint}} \sim K_{\mathrm{eff}} \cdot \eta_{\max}$) provides the structural rationale: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ decomposes joint vulnerability into a diversity factor and a per-judge capability ceiling, a structure $\kappa$-diversity lacks (Appendix~\ref{app:alt_diversity}).

